1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1068104a-c084-560a-bbeb-c3ccd952752a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1068104a-c084-560a-bbeb-c3ccd952752a" :answer-field/id #uuid "1068104a-c084-560a-bbeb-c3ccd952752a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9eeeff2c-a26a-5efe-999b-770dee902f46" "answer-c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" "answer-9cee02f8-68cf-5851-8f1a-97dfedd2201f" "answer-bd58722b-d78b-5b4d-ac72-d93c0e1f07e9" "answer-a027a479-344f-5843-8902-bf22751ea32f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9eeeff2c-a26a-5efe-999b-770dee902f46" :answer/id #uuid "9eeeff2c-a26a-5efe-999b-770dee902f46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" :answer/id #uuid "c7d9e5de-adc7-5e5d-8e0c-0e55b15b1b69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cee02f8-68cf-5851-8f1a-97dfedd2201f" :answer/id #uuid "9cee02f8-68cf-5851-8f1a-97dfedd2201f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd58722b-d78b-5b4d-ac72-d93c0e1f07e9" :answer/id #uuid "bd58722b-d78b-5b4d-ac72-d93c0e1f07e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a027a479-344f-5843-8902-bf22751ea32f" :answer/id #uuid "a027a479-344f-5843-8902-bf22751ea32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186321065 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186321061 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186319174 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" :question/id #uuid "6f3e3a92-ac43-52ed-a6de-e1847c3b16d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-35300" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a08b0b02-a55d-50fe-a6cc-f8c71f19c1af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a08b0b02-a55d-50fe-a6cc-f8c71f19c1af" :answer-field/id #uuid "a08b0b02-a55d-50fe-a6cc-f8c71f19c1af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-fie